  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through next Friday)\nIssued at 1255 PM CDT Fri Jul 10 2026\n\nThe forecast for next week looks much more active with higher rain \nchances and cooler than normal temperatures.  Models remain in good \nagreement that the upper-level ridge will shift into the northern \nPlains and strengthen. Meanwhile a broad area of mid-level vorticity \nwill rotate around the base of this ridge and into central Texas \nthrough Monday. Surface winds should become more southeasterly and \ncontinue to advect gulf moisture into west central Texas Sunday \nthrough Wednesday.  PWAT values will range from 1.5-1.7 inches \nduring this time with dewpoints in the mid 60s. Low to medium (20-\n40%) chances for scattered showers will spread across west central \nTexas by Sunday night. A surface cold front will also drop into \nTexas on Monday, which should provide a good source of lift for more \ncoverage of showers and thunderstorms through Tuesday. Chances for \nrain will increase to medium to high (50-75%) range for Monday and \nTuesday. At this time, overall rainfall amounts do not look \nsignificant. However, any area that experiences multiple rounds of \nstorms could see locally heavy rainfall.  Based on how the pattern \nis trending, Monday and Tuesday have the potential to see the \nheaviest rainfall amounts.\n\nThe front should wash out by Wednesday but moisture and upper-level \ntroughing should still be over west central Texas.  As it stands, \nthis means low (<30%) chances for scattered showers will linger into \nWednesday before drying up on Thursday.",
